21:26:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A very clear place to look is: **At night, stand with a bright white streetlamp behind you and look into the mist from a sprinkler or fountain in front of you.** For example: 1. Find a park, garden, or lawn with a **sprinkler running at night**. 2. Stand so a **bright LED streetlamp** is directly behind your head or shoulders. 3. Look toward the spray, especially at droplets several meters away. 4. Move your head slightly up/down or side-to-side. You may see a small colored arc—or more often a colored circular patch—centered around the point directly opposite the lamp. The strongest part is usually about **42° away from that opposite point**, just like a solar rainbow. A particularly easy related phenomenon is a **“glory” or halo around your shadow** in fine mist: if you shine a bright white flashlight or car headlight from behind yourself into mist, you can sometimes see colored rings centered on the shadow of your head. That is not exactly the same optical mechanism as a normal rainbow, but it demonstrates how water droplets plus artificial light can produce color patterns. Why you probably have not noticed a proper streetlamp rainbow: - Most nighttime rain is too dark and unevenly lit. - Streetlights are often yellowish or have multiple lamps shining from different directions. - The rainbow is faint and may only occupy a small part of the spray. - You need the lamp, your eye, and the droplets in the right geometry at the same time. **Best practical setup:** a white LED floodlight or car headlights behind you, with a garden hose set to a fine spray in front of you. In darkness, that arrangement can produce a visible small rainbow much more reliably than ordinary rain under a streetlamp.
